Newport framed console asset
============================

sgiconsole.png is the background supplied for this feature.  Its native size
is 1282x1024; the final two columns are black.  NEWPORT_FRAME uses the image
at 1282x1024 or clips those columns at 1280x1024.  Other display sizes retain
the normal NEWPORT_DECOR gradient and full-screen text area.

The text rectangle is (288,176) through (991,847), inclusive: 704x672 pixels,
or 88 columns by 42 rows with the normal 8x16 font.  It lies entirely inside
the uniform grey panel, leaving padding between text and the bevel.

Regenerate the checked-in newport_frame.h from this directory with:

    python3 mkframe.py
    python3 mkframe.py --check

Pillow is needed for regeneration only.  Kernel builds use the generated
header and do not need Python, Pillow, the PNG, or a runtime image decoder.
The header records the source SHA256 and encodes disjoint filled rectangles.
The converter verifies that they reconstruct every source pixel exactly.

All 161 source colours are preserved.  The driver reserves the RGB332 text
colours and their inverse cursor colours, plus the grey panel's inverse.
Only the panel and text colours can appear under a bounded text cursor, so
the other image colours do not need inverse entries.  The converter permits
at most 223 image colours to leave room for those reservations.

Changing the image requires reviewing the text rectangle in mkframe.py.
It must remain within a uniform panel: early hardware scrolling and the
fast cell-background fill depend on that property.  A background with a
pattern beneath the text would require restoring that pattern per cell and
deferring decorated scrolling until vcons has saved characters to redraw.

Runtime selection
-----------------

NEWPORT_DECOR also accepts themes loaded by wsdecorctl without rebuilding the
kernel.  The startup theme is share/wscons/themes/framed.conf; its image is
converted by share/wscons/themes/mkwsd.py.  The generated header above is only
needed for the optional compiled-in NEWPORT_FRAME appearance.  Runtime theme
configuration and testing are described in tests/dev/wscons/README.runtime-themes
and wsdecorctl(8).
